You can save the extra read in `createPayment`. PutItem accepts `ReturnValuesOnConditionCheckFailure = ALL_OLD`: when the condition fails, the exception carries the item that made it fail, and AWS charges no read units for it. With the raw client:

```kotlin
try {
    low.putItem { it.tableName(table).item(claimItem)
        .conditionExpression("attribute_not_exists(pk)")
        .returnValuesOnConditionCheckFailure(ReturnValuesOnConditionCheckFailure.ALL_OLD) }
} catch (e: ConditionalCheckFailedException) {
    val existing = e.item()   // the claim that won, from the same round trip
}
```

DynamoDB Local 3.3.2 returns the item here too. The lab keeps the explicit consistent read because it's easier to follow.

Secondary indexes come in two kinds. A local secondary index (LSI) keeps the table's partition key and adds a different sort key, so it's another ordering of the same item collection. You can only create LSIs with the table, at most five, and a table with LSIs caps each item collection at 10 GB. A global secondary index (GSI) has its own partition key, can be added at any time, and is updated asynchronously, which is why its reads are only eventually consistent. Since November 2025 a GSI key can be made of several attributes, up to four for the partition key and four for the sort key, so you no longer have to glue `status#createdAt` into one string. Lesson 4 builds the payments GSIs.

TTL deletions show up in DynamoDB Streams as service deletions, not user deletes, so a stream consumer can tell an expired claim from one your code removed.
